kgf 0.6
language: plaintext
sources: .txt, .text, .rst, .adoc

=== lex
SKIP /[ \t]+/
# A run of line breaks is one NL token, so a blank line does not become two.
# The `+` has to repeat the whole `\r\n` pair, not just the `\n`: with
# `/\r?\n+/` a CRLF blank line (`\r\n\r\n`) matched only the first pair and
# stopped at the second `\r`, handing the grammar two NL tokens in a row and
# ending the parse at the first paragraph of every CRLF file.
TOKEN NL /(\r?\n)+/
TOKEN Text /[^\r\n]+/

=== grammar
# A document is a run of blocks, and a block is a line of text with the line
# break that ends it. The trailing NL is optional so the last line of a file
# that does not end in a newline still parses; the bare `NL` alternative
# accepts a leading or repeated break that no text precedes, so a file that
# opens with a blank line is read to its end rather than stopping at line 1.
Doc -> Block*
Block -> Text NL? / NL

=== attrs

=== features
content_type: document
coverage_token_kinds: Text
vocabulary_token_kinds: Text

